 First, multiply the numerators and denominators under the radicals, and then simplify.  $\frac{\sqrt{2 \cdot 4 \cdot 6}}{\sqrt{3 \cdot 5 \cdot 7}} = \frac{\sqrt{48}}{\sqrt{105}}$. The denominator is not rationalized yet, so I will multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\sqrt{105}$ and simplify.  \[
\frac{\sqrt{48}}{\sqrt{105}} = \frac{\sqrt{48} \cdot \sqrt{105}}{\sqrt{105} \cdot \sqrt{105}} = \frac{\sqrt{48 \cdot 105}}{105} = \frac{\sqrt{5040}}{105}
\] The number under the radical is not nice, so I will try to simplify it.  The prime factorization of 5040 is $2^4 \cdot 3^2 \cdot 5 \cdot 7$, where several factors are perfect squares.  \[
\frac{\sqrt{5040}}{105} = \frac{\sqrt{2^4 \cdot 3^2 \cdot 5 \cdot 7}}{105} = \frac{2^2 \cdot 3 \cdot \sqrt{5 \cdot 7}}{105} = \boxed{\frac{12\sqrt{35}}{105}} = \boxed{\frac{4\sqrt{35}}{35}}
\] </think> <answer> $\boxed{\frac{4\sqrt{35}}{35}}$ </answer>

